From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:04:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] media: i2c: ov13858: Limit the analogue gain to the range the
 sensor follows

The analogue gain control ranges from 0 to 0x1fff in steps of 1/128. On
a Surface Pro 9 the sensor follows the register from 0x80 (1x) to 0x7c0
(15.5x). 0x7c0 to 0x7ff give the same image, 0x800 and 0x1000 give that
of 0x400, and 0x1fff that of 0x7ff: the bits above bit 10 add nothing.
Below 0x80 the gain does not go under 1x and is not monotonic (0x40
gives a brighter image than 0x80).

An auto exposure loop that believes in a 64x maximum steps through
values whose gain falls back by half, and cannot settle in dim light.

Make the range 0x80 to 0x7c0.

Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/media/i2c/ov13858.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/i2c/ov13858.c b/drivers/media/i2c/ov13858.c
index e23e8362c..772c9297f 100644
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/ov13858.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/ov13858.c
@@ -63,8 +63,13 @@
 
 /* Analog gain control */
 #define OV13858_REG_ANALOG_GAIN		0x3508
-#define OV13858_ANA_GAIN_MIN		0
-#define OV13858_ANA_GAIN_MAX		0x1fff
+/*
+ * 1/128 steps. The sensor follows the value from 1x (0x80) to 15.5x (0x7c0).
+ * Values below 0x80 do not give less than 1x, and the bits above bit 10 do
+ * not add gain: 0x800 and 0x1000 give the gain of 0x400.
+ */
+#define OV13858_ANA_GAIN_MIN		0x80
+#define OV13858_ANA_GAIN_MAX		0x7c0
 #define OV13858_ANA_GAIN_STEP		1
 #define OV13858_ANA_GAIN_DEFAULT	0x80
 
